\section{Cut Commits to Choices Already Made}

A toy compiler driver may list all supported back ends or pick one default. Those are different interfaces. The following Prolog code deliberately includes two versions of the default operation:

\begin{verbatim}
candidate(native, c).
candidate(native, llvm).
candidate(wasm, wasm).

first_naive(Target, Backend) :-
    candidate(Target, Backend),
    !.

default_backend(Target, Backend) :-
    once(candidate(Target, Chosen)),
    Backend = Chosen.
\end{verbatim}

A direct cut (\verb|!|) succeeds immediately but discards the alternatives created since this invocation of its containing predicate began, including alternatives for goals to its left and later clauses for the invocation. It does not discard the caller's older choice points. Goals to its right may still create new choices. Luger and Stubblefield illustrate this with a search in which a cut freezes the first move while leaving alternatives for a later move. Holm's Scheme presentation also treats pruning separately from ordinary conjunction and disjunction.

With \texttt{native} supplied and the second argument unbound, both default implementations report \verb|B = c|. Calling \verb|first_naive(native, llvm)|, however, binds the second argument before \texttt{candidate} is called. Only the \texttt{llvm} fact matches, and cut commits to it. Calling \verb|default_backend(native, llvm)| instead fails. \texttt{once/1} seeks the first success of its argument goal and suppresses its remaining answers; the fresh \texttt{Chosen} ensures the request is checked against the default \emph{after} the choice. This driver documents the mode with \texttt{Target} provided. It does not offer this predicate as an enumerator of defaults across multiple targets.

The cut in \texttt{first\_naive/2} is not a mere optimization of the candidate relation: it removes the second answer when the output is free. A \emph{green cut} is one that prunes branches without changing the intended answers for the supported calls, for instance when the engine would otherwise retry unreachable clauses. Such a claim depends on the mode and the other clauses. Adding a fallback fact or allowing more general queries can invalidate it, so test those assumptions rather than naming every cut in a deterministic-looking predicate green.

