\section{Walkthrough: Save a Boundary Before Committing}

For a direct cut in an ordinary clause body, the following pseudocode uses conceptual choice-stack depths. The second procedure makes the boundary of the first-answer operation explicit.

\begin{verbatim}
enter_predicate:
    cut_boundary := current choice-stack depth
    # later clause and subgoal alternatives are above this depth
execute_direct_cut:
    discard alternatives above cut_boundary
    succeed                            # keep current bindings

first_success(goal):
    boundary := current choice-stack depth
    run goal until it fails or succeeds
    on success: discard alternatives above boundary
    return goal status
\end{verbatim}

Trace \verb|default_backend(native, llvm)|: the first candidate binds \texttt{Chosen} to \texttt{c}. The \texttt{once} boundary is older than the pending \texttt{llvm} candidate, which is discarded. The comparison \verb|llvm = c| then fails. The invariant is that a commit preserves the bindings of the selected success and the ability to retry preexisting caller alternatives; it does not itself roll back either. If the caller uses \verb|(default_backend(native, llvm) ; B = fallback)|, the right-hand disjunct is still available. \Cref{fig:visual-341} puts the two alternatives on opposite sides of the boundary.

