\section{Negation Tests for Finite Failure}

Prolog's \verb|\+ G| attempts \texttt{G}. The first success of \texttt{G} makes the test fail; finite failure of \texttt{G} makes the test succeed. If the attempt diverges, the test can diverge too. The test exports no bindings from \texttt{G}. It is not classical negation and does not enumerate the complement of a relation.

\begin{verbatim}
blocked(wasm).

available(Target, Backend) :-
    candidate(Target, Backend),
    \+ blocked(Backend).
\end{verbatim}

Here all candidate facts are ground, so the negative goal probes a particular back end. The bare query \verb|?- \+ blocked(B).| fails because some back end is blocked. It does not yield \texttt{B} values other than \texttt{wasm}. Swapping the two body goals would change the executable behavior for an unbound \texttt{Backend}. The data also have to be complete for the intended policy: if an entry is missing because a configuration file was not read, treating absence as permission would be wrong.

For this driver the complement is relative to the separately listed candidates. Adding \verb|blocked(llvm).| removes a previously available answer without touching the candidate facts. That is a nonmonotone policy, unlike the positive rules used for reachability later in this part. An alternative interface is to store explicit status facts when an unknown status must not be confused with permission. It can then distinguish ``blocked'', ``allowed'', and ``no status available'' without asking Prolog to infer a classical negative proposition.

\section{Backtracking Does Not Rewind the Outside World}

In SWI-Prolog, \verb|format/2| writes output immediately:

\begin{verbatim}
report_llvm(Target, Backend) :-
    candidate(Target, Backend),
    format('trying ~w~n', [Backend]),
    Backend = llvm.
\end{verbatim}

For \verb|?- report_llvm(native, B).| it prints \texttt{trying c} and then \texttt{trying llvm}, even though only the latter branch returns an answer. Undoing a variable binding does not erase printed bytes. Running a side-effecting goal inside negation does not erase its effects either. A driver that wants a report only for the selected result should first obtain the result through a query without those effects, then report it once outside the search. Tests can check the answer substitution and the emitted log independently.

The same boundary matters when a driver updates a configuration table: ordinary Prolog database updates do not automatically roll back on failure. Rather than using a failed branch to represent a dry run, compute a list of proposed changes as data. A caller can inspect or discard that list, or apply it after committing to a selected build plan.

This gives a compiler driver a useful output discipline: search produces an inert plan, and a separate committed phase performs logging, file writes, or configuration updates exactly once.
